# Migration chain.
#
# The runtime migrator reads migrations/runtime/meta/_journal.json with
# JSON.parse, which throws on a leading UTF-8 BOM. A BOM there is not a
# cosmetic problem: it aborts readMigrationFiles before a single statement
# runs, so the whole chain silently stops applying while the schema and the
# journal both still look correct. See c7f92fce9.
#
# Pinning these to LF keeps PowerShell rewrites (Out-File / Set-Content
# default to UTF-8 *with* BOM on Windows) from churning line endings on top
# of that. Note that eol=lf normalizes line endings only -- Git has no
# attribute that strips a BOM, so the actual BOM guard is
# `npm run check:migration-journal`, wired into `npm run verify`.
migrations/** text eol=lf
*.sql text eol=lf

# Scaffold templates.
#
# Same class of problem as the migration chain above, one step removed: these
# .hbs files are rendered into TypeScript source, and the scaffold tests assert
# against that output with LF literals (e.g. `pgTable(\n  "..."`). Checked out
# with CRLF on Windows, the generated source contains \r\n and those assertions
# cannot match — server/domain/scaffold/tests/dataset-pack.test.ts failed for
# exactly this reason, on Windows only, while CI on Linux stayed green.
packages/scaffold-registry/data/** text eol=lf
